1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
One latest event per ticket. Number each ticket's events, keep number 1, and keep every ticket.

2
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,200
All 21 events, in the order they were recorded.

3
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:13,800
PARTITION BY ticket_id: each ticket's events are numbered separately.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:18,200
ORDER BY event_at DESC puts the newest event first inside each ticket.

5
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,700
Ticket 1003: 5007 gets 1, 5006 gets 2, 5005 gets 3.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,200
Nothing is filtered yet: still 21 rows. rn=1 marks the 12 we will keep.

7
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:26,700
Unlike GROUP BY, the original rows survive, so we can pick a whole event row.

8
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:33,200
The notebook's demo: two invented rows in a temporary table, then ROLLBACK.

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:39,200
Same timestamp. Ordered by event_at alone, neither row is guaranteed to come first.

10
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:42,700
The larger event_id wins the tie: 9902 gets rn = 1, 9901 gets 2.

11
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,700
It is a reporting rule you declare. A larger ID need not mean a later event.

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:47,700
The demo rolls back: the published 21 events never change.

13
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:55,700
WHERE runs before window functions are computed, so rn doesn't exist yet.

14
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,900
So rank in one query, then filter in the query around it.

15
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:01,900
12 rows, one per ticket. 1003 now shows only 5007.

16
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:03,900
ticket_events itself is untouched: it still holds all 21 records.

17
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:10,700
tickets decides which rows exist. status is the ticket's current status.

18
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:12,700
Unassigned 1004 and 1009 stay, with events 5008 and 5016.

19
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:16,200
A required join to users here would make both of them vanish.

20
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:21,200
This fixture has none, but a ticket with no events would keep NULL event columns.

21
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:25,200
An inner join, or rn = 1 moved into the final WHERE, would drop that ticket.

22
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:28,200
Check it: 12 rows, 12 distinct IDs, and 1003's pick matches its own history.

23
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:40,800
Rank, filter, then join from tickets: ROW_NUMBER keeps every row and adds a position: still 21 events. PARTITION BY ticket_id restarts the numbering for each ticket. event_id DESC breaks timestamp ties: a rule, not proof of real order. Filter rn = 1 in an outer query; the same SELECT can't see rn yet. Start FROM tickets and LEFT JOIN: all 12 requests stay, 1004 and 1009 too.

